We examine a three-dimensional system of differential equations given by 

\begin{equation*}
\begin{array}{r}
x^\prime = y + z + \varepsilon \left( 1 - 2x \right) \\
y^\prime = 1 - 2x \cdot y \\
z^\prime = 1 - 2x \cdot z \\
\end{array}
\end{equation*}

where ε is a small parameter. This system has complex dynamics and exhibits a rich behavior as the parameter ε changes. Specifically, we find that for ε within a certain range, there exist non-standard heteroclinic loops that are not aligned with the main flow of the system. We identify the stable tangents to these loops and provide explicit formulas for their parametrization. Our results are derived using advanced analytical techniques, including the spectral decomposition of linearized maps and the application of the implicit multiplier method. We also discuss the system's global dynamics and provide detailed numerical simulations to support our findings. The system under investigation is inspired by the three-dimensional R\"ossler system, a well-known model in nonlinear dynamics. We speculate that similar systems may arise naturally in other contexts within the field of nonlinear science. Our research has important implications for the understanding of chaotic systems and may be applied to the analysis of related problems in nonlinear dynamics. Furthermore, we recognize the possibility of extending our findings to more complex systems of higher dimensionality. Consequently, we anticipate that our research will contribute to the ongoing efforts to develop a comprehensive theory of complex dynamical behavior in nonlinear systems. Ultimately, we aim to contribute to the advancement of our understanding of chaotic systems and their complex behavior. Our research has been conducted in collaboration with Dr. Zofia Kopczynska. The research was carried out within the framework of a project financed by the National Science Centre, Republic of Poland. The findings presented in this paper are an extension of previous studies on related problems. Our research has been made possible by the financial support of the National Science Centre, Republic of Poland, under grant agreement No. 2016/19/HS/Z}. We would like to express our gratitude to the National Science Centre, Republi